          <article class="archive-item" id="post-27607">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-07">7 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>Claude Code&#x27;s “Don&#x27;t Ask Again” File Exposes Passwords and Keys in 1,122 Public Copies on GitHub</h3>
            <p>Developers using Claude Code may expose passwords and keys in public GitHub repositories due to hardcoded secrets in project files.</p>
            <p>The issue with Claude Code is real and concerning. Developers are inadvertently exposing secrets by approving commands that include passwords and tokens. These are saved in plain text in a project file, and they end up in public GitHub repos. It&#x27;s a clear risk to security and privacy.</p>
            <p>The problem isn&#x27;t just about the file being public. Older versions stay in git history, and forks spread the exposure. Even if you delete the file, the secrets remain. This means you have to rotate all credentials first, then clean up.</p>
            <p>The solution requires both education and process. Developers need to avoid hardcoding secrets in commands. Instead, use variables from a secrets manager. Also, approve patterns rather than single commands. Security teams must treat agent files like .env files and scan them in CI and pre-commit.</p>
            <p>This isn&#x27;t a one-off issue. It&#x27;s a symptom of a broader challenge in AI-assisted coding. We need better safeguards and clearer guidance. Developers must be trained to think securely from the start.</p>
          </article>
