          <article class="archive-item" id="post-27255">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-06">6 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>Malicious Browser Extensions Could Use Claude Dispatch to Run Code on Users&#x27; Computers</h3>
            <p>A malicious browser extension could exploit Claude Desktop&#x27;s Dispatch feature to run code on users&#x27; computers by impersonating their input.</p>
            <p>The article highlights a critical security flaw in Claude Desktop through its Dispatch feature. A malicious browser extension could exploit this to run code on a user&#x27;s computer by impersonating their input. The attack relies on Dispatch, which allows tasks to be sent from a phone to the desktop app. This creates a pathway for unauthorized actions, such as installing packages or accessing files, without needing a browser exploit.</p>
            <p>This underscores the need for strict access controls and continuous monitoring of AI-powered tools. AI agents like Claude Desktop have significant permissions, so their behavior must be scrutinized. Organizations should treat these tools with the same rigor as traditional software, ensuring they are authorized, audited, and monitored for unexpected activity.</p>
            <p>The researchers noted that Dispatch is being retired, but the underlying design remains a risk. Other features like Code Remote Control and Cowork sessions could enable similar attacks. This reinforces the importance of securing all AI agent integrations, especially when they interact with browser extensions or other third-party tools. It&#x27;s not just about the tool itself but the ecosystem around it.</p>
            <p>Securing agentic AI requires a proactive approach. Teams must implement strict access controls, monitor for unusual activity, and ensure that all integrations are vetted. As AI adoption grows, so does the attack surface. We must stay ahead by embedding security into every layer of the stack, from the browser to the desktop app.</p>
          </article>
