            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-05">5 November 2026</time> &middot; on Zerobec</p>
            <h3>PhantomDOM: Microsoft 365 Browser-in-the-Middle Phishing</h3>
            <p>PhantomDOM exploits browser-in-the-middle techniques to hijack Microsoft 365 sessions through custom DOM mirroring and WebSocket interactions.</p>
            <p>PhantomDOM represents a new frontier in browser-in-the-middle attacks, leveraging custom DOM mirroring to hijack Microsoft 365 sessions. The attack begins with a SharePoint-themed PDF, followed by browser fingerprinting and anti-bot checks. The victim is then presented with a familiar sign-in experience, but the session runs in an attacker-controlled remote browser. This bypasses traditional detection methods that rely on cookie-based replay attacks.</p>
            <p>The implementation uses WebSocket for real-time interaction, including keystroke and clipboard capture, while maintaining session continuity through reconnection logic. The attacker can monitor or take control of the session, effectively granting access to enterprise resources like Microsoft 365 Search and Copilot Chat. This exposure allows for rapid mapping of business context and potential document staging under a trusted identity.</p>
            <p>For enterprise security, this highlights the need for stronger browser fingerprinting, anti-bot checks, and secure session management. Traditional defenses are no longer sufficient. We must evolve our approach to protect against these sophisticated attacks. The implications are clear: a hijacked session can lead to immediate access to sensitive data and AI-powered workflows.</p>
          </article>
