            <p>ARTEX and its forks are developing advanced methods to detect honeypots using static fingerprints, behavioral checks, and egress validation to distinguish real systems from decoys.</p>
            <p>ARTEX and its forks are evolving to detect honeypots with sophisticated, layered strategies. This isn&#x27;t just about avoiding traps—it&#x27;s about shaping how AI-driven penetration testing operates. The agent&#x27;s plan includes static fingerprints, behavioral checks, and egress validation, all designed to distinguish real systems from decoys.</p>
            <p>The design is meticulous. It uses known defaults, like SSH banners and certificate subjects, to flag potential honeypots. These signatures are tied to public sources, ensuring transparency and reducing false positives. The system also monitors internal consistency, such as file timestamps and memory info, to catch synthetic responses.</p>
            <p>What’s striking is the emphasis on agent safety. The code actively avoids counter-attack types and refrains from exploiting confirmed decoys. It prioritizes minimizing exposure over aggressive tactics, recognizing that false positives can waste time and resources. The framework also includes defenses against AI-specific honeypots, like attestation requests and prompt injection traps.</p>
            <p>This is a clear signal for defenders. Decoys must be carefully crafted to avoid triggering these checks. Default configurations, static responses, and predictable behavior are now potential liabilities. The best decoys will blend in, avoid fingerprinting, and resist behavioral analysis. For attackers, the challenge is not just in evading detection but in maintaining credibility without revealing their true intent.</p>
