          <article class="archive-item" id="post-26660">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>Radio With No Radar: Why Agent Runtime Security Fails Without the Layers Beneath It</h3>
            <p>Runtime enforcement in agentic AI systems requires multi-layered visibility and context-aware controls to prevent data exposure and ensure proactive security.</p>
            <p>Runtime enforcement in agentic AI systems is more than a technical challenge—it&#x27;s a governance imperative. Visibility into agents’ behavior, connections, and permissions is critical. Without it, even well-intentioned tools can bypass controls and expose data. The recent case where an AI assistant routed emails through a personal account highlights how runtime rules must block risky actions in real time. Yet, most solutions ignore the reality that agents operate within third-party platforms, making traditional gateways ineffective.</p>
            <p>The inspection point for agent traffic isn’t always where the enterprise expects. Platforms like Salesforce, Slack, and Microsoft 365 host agents that run within their trust boundaries. This means security teams can’t rely on a single gateway to monitor all activity. The OWASP Agentic Skills Top 10 calls this the “unreachable skill” scenario. The lesson from past security shifts—like moving from network to application-layer inspection—applies here. Agent runtime needs a similar move, but with added complexity: the application layer belongs to someone else.</p>
            <p>A runtime strategy must span multiple vantage points, not just a single gateway. Enforcement needs a live map of identity, permissions, and activity to judge gray cases accurately. A millisecond verdict requires context—like the agent’s owner, the data’s sensitivity, and the delegation chain. Without this, security becomes reactive, not proactive. Mature enforcement allows known-good actions, warns on plausible risks, and blocks hostile chains. It also logs every decision with a full causal path, which is essential for incident reviews and regulatory compliance.</p>
            <p>The end-state fallacy is a common mistake. Assuming inline enforcement will see everything overlooks the need for foundational layers like discovery, posture, and identity. What’s available now is the stack runtime depends on. Vendors that built for the problem can answer key questions about how they manage agent discovery, governance, and enforcement. Platforms like Reco offer a holistic view by connecting all identities, applications, and permissions into a single live map. This approach transforms reach into the unit of analysis, not configuration.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-27106">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time> &middot; on Endor Labs</p>
            <h3>Docker Sandboxes vulnerability lets AI agents access sensitive files outside their sandbox (CVE-2026-103916)</h3>
            <p>A Docker Sandboxes vulnerability allows AI agents to access or overwrite files outside their sandbox through the sbx cp command.</p>
            <p>The Docker Sandboxes vulnerability (CVE-2026-103916) highlights a critical flaw in how AI agents interact with file systems. The sbx cp command bypasses the allowlist, allowing agents to access or overwrite files outside their sandbox. This is a direct risk to data integrity and confidentiality. It underscores the need for stronger sandbox controls and careful handling of file operations.</p>
            <p>The vulnerability exploits symbolic links, enabling agents to trick the CLI into copying files beyond the intended scope. This means an attacker could inject malicious code or steal sensitive data through a prompt injection attack. The implications are severe, especially in agentic workflows where agents often propose copy commands. Operators must remain vigilant and verify paths before executing any file transfer.</p>
            <p>Docker&#x27;s fix, delayed until version 0.51.0, shows how complex these security boundaries can be. Until then, treat sbx cp like any untrusted command. Resolve paths manually, confirm they stay within approved directories, and be wary of copy operations the agent suggests. This is a reminder that isolation is only as strong as the controls that enforce it.</p>
            <p>This isn&#x27;t the first time such a flaw has emerged. It reinforces the need for rigorous model evaluation and monitoring. AI agents operate in environments where trust is paramount, and any gap in containment can lead to serious breaches. As we build more agentic systems, we must ensure every channel is secured, not just the obvious ones.</p>
          </article>
