            <h3>What CISOs should know about SaaS security in the age of AI</h3>
            <p>SaaS security faces new risks from AI integration including expanded access, nonhuman identities, and prompt injection requiring updated governance and monitoring.</p>
            <p>SaaS security is evolving rapidly with the integration of AI. The old questions about access and configuration still matter but now carry new weight. AI agents can act on behalf of users in ways that expand risk exposure. A simple CRM access might now mean an agent can process thousands of records across multiple systems. This changes the potential impact of excessive privilege.</p>
            <p>The visibility challenge is also growing. Vendors often add AI features through updates, blurring the line between approved SaaS and approved AI. Security teams must now inventory not just applications, but also embedded AI, models, and nonhuman identities. These elements create new attack surfaces that traditional tools may not cover.</p>
            <p>AI introduces risks like nonhuman identities, over-permissioning, and prompt injection. Agents often inherit user privileges, which is convenient but dangerous. A compromised agent can escalate access quickly. Prompt injection can manipulate AI into exposing data or triggering unintended actions. These issues demand a shift in how we govern and monitor AI-enabled SaaS.</p>
            <p>CISOs need to extend existing governance models to cover AI in SaaS. Every AI-enabled offering should have clear ownership, data classification, and permission controls. Visibility into agent behavior and data flow is critical. By applying familiar security principles to AI systems, we can manage risk without stifling innovation.</p>
