            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>PEDM vs. Standing Elevated Privileges: Control Access Without Flooding the Helpdesk</h3>
            <p>Privilege elevation management reduces security risks by limiting elevated access to specific actions and times instead of granting continuous elevated privileges.</p>
            <p>Privilege elevation and delegation management (PEDM) offers a smarter way to control access without flooding the helpdesk. Instead of granting standing elevated privileges, PEDM elevates specific actions for a defined time and under strict rules. This approach reduces the attack surface by limiting elevated access to only what&#x27;s needed, aligning with Zero Trust principles. It ensures that privileges exist only when a legitimate action requires them, not as a default for users or systems.</p>
            <p>Standing privileges create unnecessary exposure. Local admin rights, broad server access, or shared accounts often remain active long after their initial purpose. This leaves endpoints vulnerable if compromised, allowing attackers to move laterally or disable controls. PEDM addresses this by separating the task from the user, focusing on the action rather than the person. This granular control improves security without disrupting workflows.</p>
            <p>Implementing PEDM requires understanding what work truly needs elevation. Start by mapping user workflows and identifying trusted actions. Define rules around specific applications, commands, or scripts, not just user roles. Use attributes like file hashes or digital signatures to narrow scope. This precision prevents over-privileged access while ensuring critical tasks can still run smoothly.</p>
            <p>PEDM is not a set-and-forget solution. Regularly review exceptions, refine rules, and adjust policies based on real-world usage. Start small, test with a representative group, and define an emergency path to maintain operations without abandoning security. The goal is to create a policy that reflects how your organization actually works, not a static list of exceptions.</p>
          </article>
