            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>Is Your SOC Ready for AI Agents? A Maturity Check Before You Deploy</h3>
            <p>AI SOC agents can enhance alert handling by providing context and evidence but require data access, written standards, and defined permissions to be effective.</p>
            <p>AI SOC agents are redefining how we handle alerts. They start at the bottom layer, alert handling, and return analyst hours that let teams work on layers above. If your MDR flags threats too slowly or hands back alerts without context, these agents can bridge the gap. They pull context from your systems and record evidence behind each conclusion, letting your team verify the work.</p>
            <p>A SOC needs more than just the right tools. It needs data access, context, and written standards. AI agents can only investigate what they can query. If they can&#x27;t access your SIEM or identity provider, they miss key details. Readiness means API access, asset context, and healthy data pipelines. A quick test: list the queries a senior analyst ran to close five recent incidents. Any query the agent can&#x27;t run is a gap to fix before a pilot.</p>
            <p>Define what a complete investigation looks like and record your baseline. A written standard helps grade the agent&#x27;s performance. Keep a list of detections turned off due to capacity issues. When an agent investigates every alert, some of those rules can come back. Track metrics like share of alerts investigated, mean time to investigate, and how often closed alerts are reopened. The survey shows an average of 75 minutes for a thorough investigation, but alerts often wait close to an hour before being opened.</p>
            <p>If your SOC struggles with delayed escalations, lack of context, or closed alerts without explanation, AI agents are a fit. But readiness depends on data access, written standards, and defined permissions. Start with read-only access for investigations, then widen permissions as confidence grows. The same rules apply as for a new human analyst. A ready SOC has these rules written down before a pilot starts.</p>
          </article>
