            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-10-28">28 October 2026</time> &middot; on Marcolancini</p>
            <h3>Security Logging in Cloud Environments - Kubernetes</h3>
            <p>The article discusses the importance of runtime sensors like Falco and Tetragon in Kubernetes environments to detect security events that bypass traditional logging mechanisms.</p>
            <p>The importance of comprehensive logging in Kubernetes environments cannot be overstated. With workloads running in ephemeral clusters, traditional cloud logs often stop at the edge of the control plane. This leaves critical security events—like kubectl exec sessions or secret reads—unlogged. Tools like Falco or Tetragon can bridge this gap by capturing runtime events that escape the API layer. These sensors watch system calls and provide visibility into actions that don’t touch the API server.</p>
            <p>Runtime sensors are essential for detecting anomalies that go unnoticed by audit logs. They track activities inside containers, such as shell access or unauthorized file reads. On managed platforms like EKS or GKE, enabling these sensors is straightforward, but custom rules for specific workloads may require additional tools. The choice between Falco and Tetragon often comes down to the level of control and the types of events you need to monitor. Both offer valuable insights, though their approaches differ in how they interact with the kernel.</p>
            <p>Even with all logs enabled, blind spots remain. For example, direct kubelet API calls bypass the API server entirely, making them invisible to standard logging. Similarly, non-standard pods and containers—like static or init containers—can evade detection by tools that rely solely on the API. These gaps highlight the need for a layered approach, combining audit logs, control plane metrics, and runtime sensors to ensure no event goes unrecorded. The goal is to create a complete picture of cluster activity that supports fast, accurate incident response.</p>
          </article>
