            <p>AI is affecting SREs by deskilling them as automation handles routine tasks leading to loss of hands-on experience and cognitive edge.</p>
            <p>AI isn&#x27;t replacing SREs. It&#x27;s deskilling them. This isn&#x27;t a new phenomenon. It&#x27;s a pattern that&#x27;s played out in industrial automation and now resurfaces in AI-driven workflows. As AI handles routine tasks like alert triage and incident resolution, SREs are losing the muscle memory needed for complex, novel outages. The irony is that the more we automate, the more we depend on the very tools that make us less prepared for the exceptions.</p>
            <p>The stakes are high. When humans only engage with the rare, complex problems, they stop building the intuition that comes from repetition and hands-on experience. This isn&#x27;t just about getting slower—it&#x27;s about losing the cognitive edge that separates a good SRE from a great one. The risk isn&#x27;t just deskilling; it&#x27;s never-skilling. Junior engineers today may never develop the foundational skills because AI handles most of the work.</p>
            <p>We need to rethink how we integrate AI. Deliberate inefficiency—letting humans handle tasks even when AI could—keeps skills sharp. And we must design systems where humans are actively engaged, not just approving recommendations. The future of SRE isn&#x27;t AI replacing humans. It&#x27;s AI keeping humans sharp, ready, and in control.</p>
          </article>
