            <h3>Cybersecurity dominates concerns among the C-suite, small businesses and the nation</h3>
            <p>Cybersecurity has become a top business risk with AI-driven attacks increasing, prompting organizations to prioritize advanced defenses and governance frameworks.</p>
            <p>Cybersecurity has moved from the periphery to the core of business strategy. What was once a technical issue is now a boardroom priority. With AI-driven attacks on the rise, organizations must rethink how they defend against these evolving threats. The stakes are higher than ever, and the cost of inaction is measured in millions.</p>
            <p>The shift in focus is clear. Cyber events are now the top global business risk, according to the 2024 Allianz Risk Barometer. This signals a strategic imperative for leaders across all sectors. As AI becomes more integrated into the attacker’s toolkit, the need for advanced defenses and governance frameworks has never been more urgent.</p>
            <p>Generative AI is changing the game. Attackers are using large language models to launch more sophisticated social engineering campaigns. Gartner predicts that by 2027, 17% of cyberattacks will involve generative AI. This means organizations must invest in tools and strategies that can detect and neutralize these emerging threats.</p>
            <p>The solution lies in a balance. While AI offers powerful capabilities for threat detection and response, it also introduces new risks. Leaders must prioritize governance, zero trust, and AI security to stay ahead. Cybersecurity is no longer just about protecting data—it’s about securing the future of business.</p>
