            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2024-09-01">1 September 2024</time> &middot; on Smsofdeath</p>
            <h3>SMS of Death</h3>
            <p>A zero-click remote DoS vulnerability in Android&#x27;s telephony framework allows malformed WAP Push SMS to crash the phone app and potentially reboot devices into a recovery loop.</p>
            <p>A zero-click remote DoS vulnerability in Android&#x27;s telephony framework highlights the risks of unchecked input in core systems. A malformed WAP Push SMS can crash the phone app, dropping active calls including emergency ones. The flaw stems from a lack of range check when casting a long to an int, leading to a negative array size and a crash. This is not a transient issue but a persistent denial of service that can reboot the device into a recovery loop, forcing users toward a factory reset.</p>
            <p>The implications for supply chain security are clear. Core frameworks must enforce rigorous input validation to prevent such vulnerabilities. Without it, attackers can exploit weaknesses in seemingly benign protocols. This flaw affects Android 12 through 16, with a fix released in October 2026 for 14 through 17. Older devices remain vulnerable, emphasizing the need for timely patching across ecosystems.</p>
            <p>Emergency services are at risk. A device under attack cannot reliably reach 000, violating regulatory obligations in Australia and beyond. Carriers are mandated to block non-functional devices, highlighting the real-world impact of such flaws. This is not just a technical issue—it’s a critical infrastructure risk that demands immediate attention.</p>
            <p>The fix involves adding a range check before casting, which Google implemented but without fully addressing the root cause. The updated approach suppresses the crash and deletes the malicious message, breaking the reboot loop. This underscores the importance of proactive defense in supply chain security. We must ensure all systems, especially those handling critical functions, are resilient against such threats.</p>
          </article>
